  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers today, with a slight chance of thunderstorms. \n\nA backdoor cold front is currently draped across the area, roughly \noriented parallel to the US 460 corridor, and has been the focus for \nscattered showers through the day so far, mainly to southeast WV, \nthe northern New River Valley and into the Roanoke Valley. \nPrecipitation will continue into the evening, as the front nearly \nstalls over the region. Some MLCAPE is present along the warm \nfrontal part of the low pressure system, which will slowly start to \nlift northward over WV and into the OH Valley, which will keep \nslight chances of thunderstorms in the west and Alleghany Highlands \narea for today.\n\nCloud cover along and behind the front will suppress afternoon high \ntemperatures in the northern half of the forecast area, mainly in \nthe low to mid 70s. South of the front, temperatures are already in \nthe upper 70s to even low 80s, so will likely see low to mid 80s for \nthose areas. Overall, these temperatures are still 5 to 15 degrees \nabove seasonal normals.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Gusty winds Friday night into Saturday morning. Below \nnormal temperatures for the weekend. Frost possible in the mountains \nSunday morning.\n\nA strong cold front and an amplifying 500mb trough will reach \nthe area by Friday. At this time, the highest potential for \nsevere thunderstorms is north of the forecast area, over MD, PA,\nand other parts of the northern Mid Atlantic, closer to the \nprimary surface low. However, cannot rule out a few \nthunderstorms, nor redevelopment of showers/storms in the \nPiedmont late Friday, as some CAMs are indicating. \n\nGusty winds will be a bigger impact to the local are, from Friday \nafternoon, peaking during the overnight hours, and then tapering off \nby mid Saturday afternoon. Could see stronger gusts linger across \nthe higher peaks of the Blue Ridge into Saturday evening.\n\nThere will be a significant cool down behind this front, the \napproaching airmass of Canadian origin will bring temperatures \nnear to below freezing, especially in the mountains. NBM \nprobabilities for temperatures below 32 deg F Sunday morning are\nabove 90% for the typical cold spots, like Burkes Garden and \nMount Rogers VA, and western Greenbrier County WV, with 45% to \n65% elsewhere in the mountains. From Friday to Saturday, could \nsee a 15 to 20 degrees swing in high temperatures. Dry weather \nand moderating temperatures will dominate the rest of the week \nas high pressure moves over the region once again.",
